API Request Structure

*​Every API request is made up of several key parts working together.
​
The Basic Flow
​1. REQUEST (Client ➔ Server): 
Consists of Endpoint + Headers + Params + Body

​2. RESPONSE (Server ➔ Client): 
Consists of Status Code + Headers + Body

​1. ENDPOINT (URL)
​Definition: The URL that identifies the resource. It is composed of the Base URL + path.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users/101](https://api.example.com/users/101)
​Base URL: [https://api.example.com](https://api.example.com)
​Path: /users/101

​2. HEADERS
​Definition: Key-value pairs that send extra information.
​Common Headers: Authorization, Accept, Content-Type.
​Example:
​Authorization: Bearer token123
​Accept: application/json
​Content-Type: application/json

​3. PATH PARAMETERS
​Definition: Values included in the URL path to identify a resource. Usually represents a unique ID.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users/101](https://api.example.com/users/101)
​Path Parameter: 101

​4. QUERY PARAMETERS
​Definition: Key-value pairs added at the end of the URL. Used for filtering, sorting, and pagination.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users?page=1&limit=10](https://api.example.com/users?page=1&limit=10)
​Query Parameters: ?page=1&limit=10

